---
import SiteLayout from "@/layouts/SiteLayout.astro";
import Hero from "@/components/blocks/Hero.astro";
import Prose from "@/components/content/Prose.astro";
import Link from "@/components/ui/Link.astro";
import { SITE } from "@/lib/site";

/** Bump when the text changes. */
const updated = "6 November 2026";
---

<SiteLayout
  title="Privacy policy"
  footer="legal"
  description="What this site stores, what it measures and who handles it. No ads, no tracking cookies, no banner."
>
  <Hero
    eyebrow="Legal"
    title="Privacy policy"
    align="center"
    lead="There are no ads, no tracking cookies and nothing sold or shared for marketing. Here is everything the site does."
  />

  <Prose class="privacy">
    <p class="text-caption privacy__updated">Last updated {updated}</p>

    <h2>Who runs it</h2>
    <p>
      I’m {SITE.name}, a Lead Product Designer, and this is my personal site. I’m responsible for
      any personal data it handles. If you have a question about it, write to me at{" "}
      <Link href={`mailto:${SITE.email}`}>{SITE.email}</Link>.
    </p>
    <p class="privacy__details">
      Dawid Młynarz UI/UX Designer
      <br />
      Chludowska 5A/2, 62-003 Biedrusko, Poland
      <br />
      NIP 6060071225
    </p>

    <h2>Hosting</h2>
    <p>
      The site is hosted on Cloudflare Pages. To deliver pages and keep the site safe from abuse,
      Cloudflare processes technical data from every request, such as your IP address, browser and
      the page you asked for. I don’t see or keep those logs. I rely on my legitimate interest in
      running a working, secure site (Article 6(1)(f) GDPR). Cloudflare is a US company and may
      process this data outside the EU. It’s certified under the EU-US Data Privacy Framework. You
      can read how Cloudflare handles the data in its{" "}
      <Link href="https://www.cloudflare.com/privacypolicy/">privacy policy</Link>.
    </p>

    <h2>Visitor statistics</h2>
    <p>
      I use Cloudflare Web Analytics to see which pages people read and roughly where visits come
      from. It doesn’t use cookies, doesn’t store anything on your device and doesn’t follow you
      across sites. I only see totals, like page views, referring sites, countries, browsers and
      device types. No individual visitor can be picked out. I rely on my legitimate interest in
      understanding how the site is used so I can improve it (Article 6(1)(f) GDPR). If you’d rather
      not be counted, an ad or tracker blocker will stop it.
    </p>

    <h2>Grist downloads</h2>
    <p>
      The site counts how often each Grist file is downloaded or installed, so I can see which drops
      are useful. Cloudflare keeps the count for me. It holds the file, the country and the kind of
      app that asked for it, like a browser or Claude Code. It doesn’t hold your IP address and
      nothing is stored on your device. If you choose Accept on the cookie notice, the copy buttons
      on a drop are counted the same way.
    </p>

    <h2>Cookies and local storage</h2>
    <p>
      Nothing the site stores needs your consent. Each item is there only to do something you asked
      for or to keep the site running.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>analytics-consent</strong> is saved in your browser’s local storage when you press
        Accept or Decline on the cookie notice, so the site doesn’t ask again on every page.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>case_study_access</strong> is a cookie set only after you enter the password for a
        protected case study, so you don’t have to type it again. It lasts 30 days and holds no
        personal data.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>theme</strong> is saved in your browser’s local storage when you switch between
        light and dark mode, so the site remembers your choice.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>nav-collapse</strong> lives in session storage for a moment while the menu animates
        between pages, and is removed as soon as the next page loads.
      </li>
      <li>
        Cloudflare may set a security cookie such as <strong>__cf_bm</strong> or
        <strong>_cfuvid</strong> to tell people from bots.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Videos and booking a call</h2>
    <p>
      The videos on the About page come from YouTube. Nothing loads from YouTube until you press
      play. Then the player loads from youtube-nocookie.com and Google’s{" "}
      <Link href="https://policies.google.com/privacy">privacy policy</Link> applies.
    </p>
    <p>
      Book a call takes you to my Google Calendar booking page. The name and email you give there
      come to me so I can set up the call. I use them to talk about working together (Article
      6(1)(b) GDPR).
    </p>

    <h2>Email</h2>
    <p>
      If you email me, I keep the conversation in my inbox so I can reply and follow up. My email
      runs on Google Workspace. I keep a conversation for up to two years after we last talked,
      unless we end up working together and the law asks me to keep it longer, for example for
      invoices. I rely on my legitimate interest in answering you (Article 6(1)(f) GDPR). I don’t
      add anyone to a mailing list.
    </p>

    <h2>Your rights</h2>
    <p>
      Under the GDPR you can ask me what I hold about you and get a copy. You can also ask me to
      correct it, delete it, limit how I use it or object to it. Just email me. If you think I’ve
      handled your data badly, you can also complain to a data protection authority. In Poland
      that’s <Link href="https://uodo.gov.pl/en">UODO</Link>.
    </p>
  </Prose>
</SiteLayout>

<style>
  @layer components {
    .privacy {
      padding-block-end: var(--space-section-md);
    }

    .privacy__details {
      color: var(--color-text-muted);
    }

    .privacy__updated {
      color: var(--color-text-muted);
    }
  }
</style>
